---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../components/work/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import CaseColophon from '../../components/work/CaseColophon.astro';
import CaseApproach from '../../components/work/CaseApproach.astro';
import RepWorkDiagram from '../../components/work/diagrams/RepWorkDiagram.astro';
import { ORGS } from '../../data/site';

// The machine-readable restatement of this page. Every claim below is made in
// plain language somewhere in the body copy.
const work = {
  name: 'AI Sales Workflow Automation, A-LIGN',
  description:
    'An AI prospecting workflow at a cybersecurity and compliance firm that looked finished and produced nothing. Michael Doerffler volunteered for it as a BDR intern, found the root cause, and rebuilt qualification in layers: accounts qualified before entry, a model qualifying contacts inside them against compliance criteria, and a rep making the final call with a written reason to override. Proven on the CMMC audit line at roughly a 1% bounce rate against a 6% team average, and now run daily by all fourteen reps on the team.',
  about: [ORGS.align],
  // Open-ended interval: the engagement is ongoing, and an end date would
  // contradict the colophon.
  temporalCoverage: '2026-05/..',
  keywords: [
    'AI workflow automation',
    'Sales operations',
    'Apollo',
    'Salesforce',
    'Outreach',
    'Prompt engineering',
    'Agentic workflow',
    'Human in the loop',
    'B2B prospecting',
    'Cybersecurity compliance',
  ],
};

const facts = [
  { label: 'ROLE', value: 'BDR intern · From filling pipeline to building the systems behind it' },
  { label: 'ORG', value: 'A-LIGN · Cybersecurity compliance' },
  { label: 'TIMELINE', value: '2026 · Ongoing' },
  { label: 'TOOLS', value: 'Apollo · Salesforce · Outreach · Agentic workflow' },
];

// What the build had to survive: five constraints, each a titled row.
const survived = [
  {
    title: 'A team of fourteen, not a workflow of one',
    body: `Fourteen personal workflows is fourteen things to maintain and fourteen ways to drift. I built one workflow per audit line feeding a shared list reps filter themselves, so there's a single place to fix and a standard everyone runs the same way.`,
  },
  {
    title: `An account layer that wasn't always there`,
    body: `When SOC 2 struggled, the layered structure told me where to look. The problem was never the prompt, it was that the qualified account list underneath it didn't exist yet.`,
  },
  {
    title: `Inventory we couldn't afford to waste`,
    body: `With CMMC paused, SOC 2 accounts got more valuable, not less. Running an untested version against them would have burned the accounts the team most needed. We paused it and reps work those by hand. That was a call made with the BDR manager, not a technical one.`,
  },
  {
    title: 'Credits that had to be spent carefully',
    body: `Volume stopped being the constraint the moment the workflow ran, since we can process as many accounts as we choose. But every enrichment run costs from a finite pool the whole org shares, so the question became how much to run rather than how much was possible. How many accounts go through a test batch before scaling, whether verification runs against a full list or only the contacts most likely to bounce, and what to tell a rep who wants more contacts when more contacts means spending the team's budget on lower-quality output. The answer is usually fewer, better, and a reason why.`,
  },
  {
    title: 'Reps who still need their own judgment',
    body: `Hand fourteen people a system that says "qualified" and they stop building the instinct to question it. The review step stays, and every contact carries a written reason a rep can read and override, even though skipping both would be faster.`,
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="AI Sales Workflow Automation · Michael Doerffler"
  description="How I rebuilt and validated a stalled AI prospecting workflow at A-LIGN, scaled it across a 14-person BDR team, and helped prepare it for broader adoption."
  ogImage="/og/prospecting-automation.png"
  breadcrumb="AI Sales Workflow Automation"
  ogType="article"
  work={work}
>
  <CaseStudyLayout
    caseLabel="A-LIGN · PROSPECTING AUTOMATION"
    eyebrow="COMPLIANCE · AI AUTOMATION"
    titleLine1="Taking the busywork out of prospecting,"
    titleLine2="without taking out the judgment."
    standfirst="I was hired to prospect. I ended up running the system the team prospects on."
    nextLabel="THE NOLL TEAM"
    nextHref="/work/noll-team/"
  >
    <CaseColophon items={facts} />

    <section class="case-section">
      <h2 class="nar-head" data-reveal>How I got involved</h2>
      <p class="situation-stmt" data-reveal>
        Projects like this were not part of a BDR intern's job. My manager mentioned a stalled
        Apollo build in a team meeting, and <span class="situation-key">I volunteered</span>.
      </p>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        The workflow was meant to find the right contact inside every target account automatically,
        the slowest part of a rep's day at about ten minutes an account. It looked finished and
        produced nothing, with no error to explain why. To be useful I had to learn three things at
        once: Apollo, the Salesforce integration the RevOps manager had built and walked me through,
        and the compliance domain itself, because you can't automate a judgment you don't
        understand.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section case-exhibit">
      <!-- visual 1: how a rep's work changed -->
      <RepWorkDiagram />
    </section>

    <section class="case-section">
      <h2 class="nar-head" data-reveal>Deciding what the AI is allowed to decide</h2>
      <p class="situation-stmt" data-reveal>
        The bug was a field nothing wrote to. Fixing it was
        <span class="situation-key">the easy half</span>.
      </p>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        I rebuilt qualification in layers. Accounts qualify before they ever enter the workflow,
        against criteria our team and A-LIGN have built over years of knowing what a real prospect
        looks like. The model qualifies contacts inside those accounts, reading a person's live
        LinkedIn against our compliance criteria. Reps make the final call, seconds instead of ten
        minutes. The contact layer took the most iteration: too loose and we waste sends on the
        wrong people, too tight and we miss the real buyer at companies that structure the role
        differently. Every contact returns a written reason a rep can override.
      </p>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        We proved it on CMMC, the Department of Defense's standard for its contractors and one of
        A-LIGN's highest-demand lines. Emails through the workflow bounced at about 1%, against a
        6% team average.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section">
      <h2 class="nar-head" data-reveal>Then almost everything changed at once</h2>
      <div class="stood-strip" data-reveal>
        <span class="stood-label">Where it stood</span>
        <span class="stood-line">Proven on CMMC, about 1% bounce against a 6% team average, running across fourteen reps.</span>
      </div>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        CMMC was paused federally and the focus moved to SOC 2, where the platform isn't yet
        streamlined into the team's day to day: integrations have gaps, key people aren't always
        available, and the accounts in play became more valuable, not less. Keeping it moving meant
        working around all of it, bridging data in with CSV imports, getting creative with Apollo's
        filtering to keep running tests, and holding the scarce accounts for reps to work by hand
        until the output earned trust.
      </p>
      <p class="situation-stmt" data-reveal>
        None of it was what I'd <span class="situation-key">designed for</span>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section">
      <h2 class="nar-head" data-reveal>What the build had to survive</h2>
      <CaseApproach workstreams={survived} numbered={false} />
    </section>

    <section class="case-section">
      <h2 class="nar-head" data-reveal>In production</h2>

      <div class="close-group" data-reveal>
        <p class="close-label">Running today</p>
        <ul class="close-list">
          <li>All fourteen reps run it day to day</li>
          <li>Trained beyond the team: reps in EMEA and the email marketing group</li>
          <li>Reply rate, meetings booked, and bounce tracked since the first run</li>
          <li>That data drives the prompt revisions and the persona rebuilds</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="close-group" data-reveal>
        <p class="close-label">Being extended</p>
        <ul class="close-list">
          <li>Each audit line gets its own account sourcing and personas; the person who buys CMMC is not the person who buys SOC 2</li>
          <li>Account lists built upstream, so the model inherits a clean starting point instead of guessing</li>
          <li>Volume is not the limit anymore; the real decisions are how much to run and which accounts deserve it</li>
          <li>With the managers: when contacts reach people, what a standard play looks like, and how hard to lean on the tool while a mid-quarter number still has to be hit</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p class="closing-note" data-reveal>
        The first broken version taught the lesson everything since has run on: judge a system by
        what it produces, not by whether it looks right.
      </p>
    </section>
  </CaseStudyLayout>
</Base>

<style>
  .case-section { margin-top: 9vh; }
  .case-exhibit { margin-top: 6vh; }

  /* .nar-head now lives in global.css, shared with the Noll case page */

  /* Pivot lines: a step below the header, a step above the body */
  .situation-stmt {
    margin: 4vh 0 0;
    max-width: 900px;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(18px, 1.8vw, 26px);
    line-height: 1.4;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .situation-key {
    color: var(--accent);
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--accent);
  }

  /* One-line proof of the working starting state */
  .stood-strip {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 18px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-top: 4vh;
    max-width: 900px;
    padding: 13px 18px;
    border: 1px solid rgba(46, 74, 58, 0.4);
    background: rgba(46, 74, 58, 0.06);
  }
  .stood-label {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--accent);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .stood-line {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(13.5px, 1.15vw, 16px);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* Standard body copy */
  .body {
    margin: 3.5vh 0 0;
    max-width: 900px;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: clamp(16px, 1.3vw, 18px);
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--ink-muted-2);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* Closer: two left-ruled bullet groups, matching the DOH questions device */
  .close-group { margin-top: 4vh; }
  .close-label {
    margin: 0 0 12px;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .close-list {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 900px;
    padding: 2px 0 2px 26px;
    border-left: 2px solid rgba(46, 74, 58, 0.35);
    list-style: none;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 11px;
  }
  .close-list li {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: clamp(15px, 1.2vw, 17px);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--ink-muted-2);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* Closing statement */
  .closing-note {
    margin: 5vh 0 0;
    max-width: 860px;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(20px, 2vw, 30px);
    line-height: 1.35;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .case-section { margin-top: 7vh; }
  }
</style>
